1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,411
Welcome to Part One of the RCC onboarding curriculum.

2
00:00:03,411 --> 00:00:09,475
This lesson is designed for biomedical researchers who may be very experienced in their scientific domain

3
00:00:09,475 --> 00:00:11,370
but new to Linux clusters.

4
00:00:11,370 --> 00:00:15,160
We will build a mental model before using any commands.

5
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:20,466
You will see what runs on your own computer, what runs on the cluster,

6
00:00:20,466 --> 00:00:26,530
why secure shell is used, how Visual Studio Code connects, how data transfer differs from interactive

7
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:29,183
login, and why Slurm controls compute jobs.

8
00:00:29,183 --> 00:00:34,489
By the end, you should be able to connect, move a small test file,

9
00:00:34,489 --> 00:00:37,900
submit a batch job, and verify where it ran.

10
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:42,070
The examples are intentionally small and contain no sensitive research data.

11
00:00:42,070 --> 00:00:47,732
Think of the RCC as a shared scientific instrument rather than a faster desktop computer.

12
00:00:47,732 --> 00:00:50,374
The login host is the reception desk.

13
00:00:50,374 --> 00:00:56,414
It authenticates you and lets you prepare work, but it is not intended for heavy analysis.

14
00:00:56,414 --> 00:01:01,321
Compute nodes are the instruments that run CPU, memory, or GPU intensive jobs.

15
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:03,963
Shared storage holds project data and results.

16
00:01:03,963 --> 00:01:10,003
Slurm is the booking and scheduling system that decides when and where a job can run.

17
00:01:10,003 --> 00:01:13,778
This separation protects other users and makes large analyses repeatable.

18
00:01:13,778 --> 00:01:19,818
A command typed in the wrong place can affect many people, so every tutorial will tell

19
00:01:19,818 --> 00:01:23,592
you where a command runs and what success looks like.

20
00:01:23,592 --> 00:01:28,111
A major source of confusion is the local and remote split.

21
00:01:28,111 --> 00:01:32,218
Your keyboard, web browser, and normal desktop applications are local.

22
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:38,790
When secure shell is connected, the terminal prompt is remote and commands execute on the RCC.

23
00:01:38,790 --> 00:01:44,541
Visual Studio Code also has two sides: the graphical application remains on your laptop,

24
00:01:44,541 --> 00:01:50,291
while its remote extension starts helper processes on the cluster and opens remote files.

25
00:01:50,291 --> 00:01:55,631
Always inspect the hostname, current directory, and prompt before deleting or moving files.

26
00:01:55,631 --> 00:02:01,792
A file in Downloads on Windows or macOS is not automatically visible on RCC storage.

27
00:02:01,792 --> 00:02:05,489
It must be transferred through an approved transfer mechanism.

28
00:02:05,489 --> 00:02:11,355
Secure Shell, usually called SSH, provides an encrypted channel between your computer and the cluster.

29
00:02:11,355 --> 00:02:15,388
An SSH key pair has a private key and a public key.

30
00:02:15,388 --> 00:02:19,787
The private key stays on your own computer.

31
00:02:19,787 --> 00:02:22,720
RCC receives only the public key.

32
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:27,653
RCC does not recommend a passphrase on the normal software-backed SSH key.

33
00:02:27,653 --> 00:02:33,519
Protect the endpoint, keep the private key local, and never share it.

34
00:02:33,519 --> 00:02:37,385
Prefer a hardware-backed FIDO SSH key where appropriate.

35
00:02:37,385 --> 00:02:42,518
Use the Windows or macOS credential manager for RCC web passwords, passkeys, and recovery material.

36
00:02:42,518 --> 00:02:45,084
Web credential storage and SSH-key policy are separate things.

37
00:02:45,084 --> 00:02:50,444
On Windows, use a current supported version with the built-in OpenSSH client.

38
00:02:50,444 --> 00:02:54,463
PowerShell is the simplest terminal for the first test.

39
00:02:54,463 --> 00:02:58,930
On macOS, the Terminal application already includes an SSH client.

40
00:02:58,930 --> 00:03:06,076
Install Visual Studio Code and Remote - SSH when you need the advanced developer path.

41
00:03:06,076 --> 00:03:11,436
First prove that the command line connection works.

42
00:03:11,436 --> 00:03:17,688
Only then configure VS Code, because the editor uses the same SSH configuration underneath.

43
00:03:17,688 --> 00:03:20,368
This makes errors easier to diagnose.

44
00:03:20,368 --> 00:03:27,514
Keep your operating system updated and do not install keys or configuration files from untrusted sources.

45
00:03:27,514 --> 00:03:32,264
The RCC connection may use a gateway before reaching the submission host.

46
00:03:32,264 --> 00:03:37,408
ProxyJump tells SSH to authenticate through the gateway while preserving end-to-end SSH behavior.

47
00:03:37,408 --> 00:03:43,345
A small configuration file gives the route a memorable alias.

48
00:03:43,345 --> 00:03:48,094
The exact hostnames and official host-key fingerprints must come from RCC administrators.

49
00:03:48,094 --> 00:03:52,447
Do not accept a changed fingerprint merely because a dialog appears.

50
00:03:52,447 --> 00:03:57,988
A changed key can indicate a rebuilt host, a configuration issue, or an attack.

51
00:03:57,988 --> 00:04:04,320
Test the route in a normal terminal and record the exact error message if it fails.

52
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:07,628
Visual Studio Code is not itself the cluster.

53
00:04:07,628 --> 00:04:14,243
The window runs locally, but Remote - SSH opens a workspace on the RCC submission host.

54
00:04:14,243 --> 00:04:18,377
A colored remote indicator shows the active connection.

55
00:04:18,377 --> 00:04:23,338
The Explorer lists remote files, and the integrated terminal starts remotely.

56
00:04:23,338 --> 00:04:28,712
Extensions may run near the files with your RCC account permissions.

57
00:04:28,712 --> 00:04:35,327
Open the intended project directory rather than your entire home directory.

58
00:04:35,327 --> 00:04:41,942
Do not launch long analyses in the integrated terminal; use Slurm.

59
00:04:41,942 --> 00:04:48,143
Use tmux only for lightweight workflow control or monitoring sessions.

60
00:04:48,143 --> 00:04:53,377
Interactive SSH is optimized for commands, not for moving large biomedical datasets.

61
00:04:53,377 --> 00:04:59,047
The approved browser transfer service provides a managed path for uploads and downloads.

62
00:04:59,047 --> 00:05:06,025
Use a project directory agreed with your group, not a random folder in your home directory.

63
00:05:06,025 --> 00:05:10,386
Transfer only data permitted by the project and institutional policy.

64
00:05:10,386 --> 00:05:15,620
For a small training file, calculate a SHA two fifty-six checksum locally,

65
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:20,853
upload the file, calculate the checksum on RCC, and compare the values.

66
00:05:20,853 --> 00:05:24,342
Matching checksums show that the bytes are identical.

67
00:05:24,342 --> 00:05:31,320
For clinical or identifiable data, follow the approved data-protection process rather than personal cloud storage or email.

68
00:05:32,629 --> 00:05:34,682
Slurm is the resource manager.

69
00:05:34,682 --> 00:05:40,840
Instead of choosing a compute node yourself, describe the resources required by a job.

70
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:43,715
Time, CPUs, memory, and sometimes a GPU.

71
00:05:43,715 --> 00:05:49,463
Slurm queues the job, finds a suitable node, starts it, records its state,

72
00:05:49,463 --> 00:05:51,105
and releases resources afterwards.

73
00:05:51,105 --> 00:05:59,727
Request realistic resources; too little can fail and too much can delay scheduling and waste capacity.

74
00:05:59,727 --> 00:06:05,476
A batch script records the command and requested resources.

75
00:06:05,476 --> 00:06:11,224
That makes work easier to repeat, audit, and share than an undocumented interactive session.

76
00:06:11,224 --> 00:06:18,185
The first job should be deliberately simple and request one CPU, modest memory, and a few minutes.

77
00:06:18,185 --> 00:06:27,344
Submit it with sbatch, observe the job identifier, and use squeue and sacct to inspect its state.

78
00:06:27,344 --> 00:06:43,832
Open the output and confirm that the work ran on a compute node; the lesson is the job lifecycle, not the arithmetic.

79
00:06:47,495 --> 00:06:53,718
A normal SSH connection ends when the laptop sleeps, the network changes, or the terminal closes.

80
00:06:53,718 --> 00:07:01,107
Tmux creates a persistent remote terminal session that you can detach and reattach.

81
00:07:01,107 --> 00:07:07,329
Use it for editing, monitoring, or a lightweight workflow controller that submits jobs to Slurm.

82
00:07:07,329 --> 00:07:15,885
Tmux does not grant compute resources or make heavy analysis acceptable on the login host.

83
00:07:15,885 --> 00:07:26,385
Tmux preserves the steering wheel while Slurm provides the engine; close sessions when work is complete.

84
00:07:26,385 --> 00:07:37,111
You are ready when you can identify local versus remote and explain private versus public SSH keys.

85
00:07:37,111 --> 00:07:44,007
You can explain that the normal RCC software-backed SSH key has no passphrase and verify the server fingerprint.

86
00:07:44,007 --> 00:07:49,753
Command-line SSH works before Visual Studio Code, if you use that advanced path.

87
00:07:49,753 --> 00:07:53,967
You can transfer a small non-sensitive file and match its checksum.

88
00:07:53,967 --> 00:08:01,245
You can submit a batch script, observe its job identifier, and confirm that it ran on a compute node.

89
00:08:01,245 --> 00:08:06,225
Keep bounded commands and logs as useful evidence when asking for support.
